  "image_name": "dc3d7557f8d0662b989c70c0362f4579.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several chemical strategies for joining or stabilizing DNA structures. Overall, it shows how duplex DNA can be connected either by enzymatic joining of natural 5′ and 3′ ends, by covalent crosslinking between strands, or by introducing nonstandard building blocks that change DNA shape and pairing.\n\nOne set of panels contrasts standard ligation with artificial crosslinking. In one case, complementary strands carrying an alkyne and an azide are linked through click chemistry to form a triazole bridge. In another, UV light induces a covalent bond between modified bases, illustrated with a thymine-like photodimer formed at about 310 nm. These methods appear to lock the duplex together without relying only on native phosphodiester chemistry.\n\nThe lower panels show additional structural modifications. Flexible terminal groups such as hexanediol or hexaethylene glycol can be attached at DNA ends, likely acting as linkers or spacers. The “L-DNA” panel compares natural D-DNA with its mirror-image form, highlighting opposite helical handedness and the different sugar stereochemistry. Finally, an “unnatural base pairs” panel depicts a noncanonical pair, labeled 5-Me-isoC and isoG, inserted alongside normal A–T and G–C pairing, emphasizing that synthetic bases can expand DNA recognition rules.",
